WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.720
During a SOC 2 audit, auditors review the organization within a defined scope.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.030
That scope identifies the system, services, criteria, control areas, report type,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.160
and review period.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.660
The audit process usually includes planning, documentation review, evidence requests,

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.100
interviews, control testing, follow-up questions, management review,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.320
and report preparation.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.010
Auditors may examine policies, procedures, access records, change tickets,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:26.730
monitoring reports, incident records, risk assessments, vendor reviews,

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.590
training completion, and other evidence connected to the control set.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:33.350
Interviews help auditors understand how processes work in practice.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.600
Personnel may be asked to explain responsibilities, workflows, tools, approval steps,

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.200
monitoring, escalation paths, or how exceptions are handled.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.425
Preparation and documentation strongly influence audit efficiency.

00:00:44.425 --> 00:00:47.880
When controls are clearly defined, owners are known, evidence is organized,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.725
and teams respond consistently, the audit tends to move more smoothly.

00:00:51.725 --> 00:00:54.735
Auditors evaluate evidence against the criteria and the organization's control

00:00:54.735 --> 00:00:55.440
descriptions.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.760
They are looking for whether controls are suitably designed and, for Type II reports,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.880
whether they operated during the review period.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.475
The audit process should not be treated as a performance.

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:06.355
It should reflect real operations.

00:01:06.355 --> 00:01:09.360
Sustainable programs make audit readiness the result of consistent work,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.145
not a separate scramble at the end.

00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:14.100
When audit questions reveal unclear ownership or missing records,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.465
the best response is usually process improvement.

00:01:16.465 --> 00:01:18.850
The objective is not just answering the current request,

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.410
but making future evidence easier to produce accurately.
